{
  "reasoning": "The earlier stages were legal and successful, but the boss line failed because turn 5 was omitted even though the enemy was still alive. The selected plays dealt 5 + 20 + 0 + 24 + 8 = 57 total damage before turn 6, leaving the boss at 15 HP. After turn 6, the model omitted turns 7 through 11 as well, so the boss continued attacking on turns 7, 9, 10, and 12 until the player died. The key missed check was whether the enemy was actually defeated on the final selected turn. A short state simulation would have shown: after turn 1 the boss has 67 HP; after turn 2, 47 HP and then gains 12 block; turn 3 removes only that block and deals no HP damage; turn 4 reduces HP to 23; turn 5 is omitted and the boss gains 12 block; turn 6 reduces HP to 15 and leaves the boss alive. A viable bounded line is to use turn 1 [shrug, iron_wave, pommel] for 14 damage and 13 block, turn 2 [twin_strike, pommel] for 23 damage, turn 3 [shrug, iron_wave, pommel] to remove the 12 block while dealing 2 damage and blocking 13, turn 4 [twin_strike, strike] for 20 damage, omit turn 5, and turn 6 [pommel, strike] for 15 damage. This defeats the boss before its turn-6 attack while taking only 15 total damage.",
  "error_identification": "The reasoning incorrectly claimed that turn 6 would finish the boss, calculating 20 damage without accounting for the boss's 12 block. In the actual execution, turn 6 dealt 9 damage to the block, 2 damage to HP with Iron Wave, and 6 more damage with Strike, leaving 15 HP. The model also failed to continue simulating after the selected plays and did not verify the required win condition before finalizing the action.",
  "root_cause_analysis": "The root cause was incomplete state tracking across turns, especially enemy block persistence into the next player turn and the distinction between damage dealt to block and damage dealt to HP. The model treated the turn-3 attack sequence as if it had meaningfully damaged the boss, although all 20 damage was absorbed by the 12 guard, and then treated turn 6 as a lethal turn without subtracting the 12 block gained on turn 5. It also optimized a plausible early sequence without checking that the full action list actually reaches a kill within the allowed turns.",
  "correct_approach": "After every selected turn, track enemy HP, enemy block, player HP, and the next intent. For each card, apply damage to enemy block before HP, then apply the enemy attack only if the enemy survives. Before returning, simulate through the first lethal turn and explicitly confirm enemy HP reaches zero. In this case, a winning line is: turn 1 play shrug, iron_wave, pommel; turn 2 play twin_strike, pommel; turn 3 play shrug, iron_wave, pommel; turn 4 play twin_strike, strike; turn 6 play pommel, strike. Turn 5 can be omitted because the enemy's guard is irrelevant if turn 6's 15 damage exactly exceeds its remaining 15 HP after the turn-4 damage.",
  "key_insight": "A legal action sequence is not sufficient: every candidate combat line must be simulated to a confirmed lethal turn. Maintain the invariant enemy_HP_after_card_resolution = enemy_HP_before - damage_after_block, and never count damage absorbed by enemy block as HP damage. In particular, when an enemy guards, plan either enough damage to remove the guard and still make progress or use defense while preparing a later lethal turn. Always verify the final selected turn kills the enemy before its next attack.",
